      2
    ],
    [
      "behtml_8",
      "Tri-Color Immutability Palette: Pure White #FFFFFF (surfaces/data entry/active content), Pure Black #000000 (toolbars, headers, grid boundaries/structural anchors), Brand Red #DE2626 (the Elton Boehnen accent - interactive highlights, technical readouts, critical alerts).",
      "keyword_only",
      true,
      2
    ],
    [
      "behtml_9",
      "Contrast laws: (1) black text is strictly prohibited on a #DE2626 background - must be white; (2) red text on black background is restricted to high-visibility technical metrics/debug footers only; (3) white backgrounds permit both black standard text and red accent text.",
      "keyword_only",
      true,
      2
    ],
    [
      "bejson_core_1",
      "BEJSON = a strict tabular JSON format with positional integrity: Fields order == every Values row's order, exactly. Spreadsheet analogy: Fields is the header row (fixed column names+order), each Values row is one spreadsheet row -- Values[row][2] is always column 3 for every row, same as cell C in row 12 is still column C. 6 mandatory keys, every file, no exceptions: Format (\"BEJSON\"), Format_Version (\"104\"|\"104a\"|\"104db\"), Format_Creator (must be exactly \"Elton Boehnen\"), Records_Type, Fields, Values. Field type must be exactly one of: string, integer, number, boolean, array, object -- lowercase, nothing else (\"Number\", \"Any\", \"str\" are all invalid). Row length must equal Fields length every time -- pad missing data with null, never shorten the row. Pick a version: 104 = one sheet, any cell type. 104a = one sheet, plain cells only, corner notes allowed. 104db = several sheets stapled into one, with a column saying which sheet each row belongs to.",
      "keyword_only",
      true,
      1
    ],
    [
      "bejson_core_2",
      "BEJSON 104 -- one plain spreadsheet tab, cells can hold anything. Records_Type = exactly ONE entity name, e.g. [\"SensorReading\"]. No custom top-level keys at all (Parent_Hierarchy is the one built-in exception, meaning is app-defined). Fields CAN be array or object type -- a cell can hold a whole list. Use for homogeneous high-throughput data: logs, metrics, archives. Example: {\"Format\":\"BEJSON\",\"Format_Version\":\"104\",\"Format_Creator\":\"Elton Boehnen\",\"Records_Type\":[\"SensorReading\"],\"Fields\":[{\"name\":\"sensor_id\",\"type\":\"string\"},{\"name\":\"temp\",\"type\":\"number\"},{\"name\":\"tags\",\"type\":\"array\"}],\"Values\":[[\"S001\",23.5,[\"indoor\"]],[\"S002\",19.8,null]]}. Note tags=null on row 2, not [] -- null means truly absent, not \"empty list\".",
      "keyword_only",
      true,
      1
    ],
    [
      "bejson_core_3",
      "BEJSON 104a -- same one-tab idea as 104, but every cell must be a plain value (string/integer/number/boolean only -- no array/object cells), and the sheet is allowed sticky-notes in the corner: custom top-level keys for FILE-level metadata only (never per-record data), PascalCase, must not collide with the 6 mandatory keys. Records_Type still exactly ONE entity name. Best for config files, settings, health checks. Example: {\"Format\":\"BEJSON\",\"Format_Version\":\"104a\",\"Format_Creator\":\"Elton Boehnen\",\"Server_ID\":\"WEB-01\",\"Records_Type\":[\"ConfigParam\"],\"Fields\":[{\"name\":\"key\",\"type\":\"string\"},{\"name\":\"value\",\"type\":\"string\"},{\"name\":\"sensitive\",\"type\":\"boolean\"}],\"Values\":[[\"db_host\",\"prod-db-01\",true],[\"max_threads\",\"32\",false]]}. \"Server_ID\" is the sticky-note; it says nothing about any individual row.",
      "keyword_only",
      true,
      2
    ],
    [
      "bejson_core_4",
      "BEJSON 104db -- several DIFFERENT tables stapled into ONE sheet. Records_Type lists 2+ entity names, e.g. [\"User\",\"Item\"]. Field 0 MUST be named exactly \"Record_Type_Parent\" (type string) -- that column's value in each row says which table this row belongs to, and must match one of the Records_Type entries. EVERY other field must carry its own \"Record_Type_Parent\": \"User\" tag saying which entity owns that column -- a field with no owner is invalid. Any column that doesn't apply to a row's entity gets null in that row (this is why 104db files balloon in size fast -- null-padding grows with every field x every record; keep 104db small, not your main database). No custom top-level keys. Convention (not enforced): foreign key columns end in _fk. Example: {\"Format\":\"BEJSON\",\"Format_Version\":\"104db\",\"Format_Creator\":\"Elton Boehnen\",\"Records_Type\":[\"User\",\"Item\"],\"Fields\":[{\"name\":\"Record_Type_Parent\",\"type\":\"string\"},{\"name\":\"username\",\"type\":\"string\",\"Record_Type_Parent\":\"User\"},{\"name\":\"name\",\"type\":\"string\",\"Record_Type_Parent\":\"Item\"},{\"name\":\"owner_id_fk\",\"type\":\"string\",\"Record_Type_Parent\":\"Item\"}],\"Values\":[[\"User\",\"alice\",null,null],[\"Item\",null,\"Report A\",\"alice\"]]}. Row 1 is a User so the Item columns are null; row 2 is an Item so username is null.",
      "keyword_only",
      true,
      2
    ],
    [
      "bejson_core_5",
      "MFDB = a library card catalog on top of BEJSON, not a new Format_Version. One manifest file (always named exactly 104a.mfdb.bejson, a BEJSON 104a doc, Records_Type=[\"mfdb\"]) is the card catalog -- it doesn't hold the data itself, it just lists where each 'book' (entity type) lives. Each entity gets its OWN separate BEJSON 104 file (default data/<entity>.bejson) -- unlike 104db, nothing is stapled together, so no null-padding between entity types. Manifest Fields required: entity_name (string, unique), file_path (string, unique, relative to manifest dir). Standard optional fields: description, record_count (advisory, not enforced), schema_version, primary_key. Example manifest: {\"Format\":\"BEJSON\",\"Format_Version\":\"104a\",\"Format_Creator\":\"Elton Boehnen\",\"MFDB_Version\":\"1.31\",\"DB_Name\":\"StoreFront\",\"Records_Type\":[\"mfdb\"],\"Fields\":[{\"name\":\"entity_name\",\"type\":\"string\"},{\"name\":\"file_path\",\"type\":\"string\"},{\"name\":\"primary_key\",\"type\":\"string\"}],\"Values\":[[\"User\",\"data/user.bejson\",\"user_id\"],[\"Order\",\"data/order.bejson\",\"order_id\"]]}.",
      "keyword_only",
      true,
      2
    ],
    [
      "bejson_core_6",
      "Each MFDB entity file is a completely normal, DENSE BEJSON 104 tab -- no structural null-padding for schema gaps (a null in an entity record means genuinely-absent data, not wrong-entity-for-this-row like in 104db). One extra required key: Parent_Hierarchy -- a return-address label, a RELATIVE path from the entity file's own folder back to the manifest (e.g. entity in data/ pointing to root manifest = \"../104a.mfdb.bejson\"). Records_Type must be exactly one string matching its entity_name in the manifest, case-sensitive. Discovery is file-local, no directory scan needed: Format_Version 104a + filename ends \".mfdb.bejson\" -> manifest. Format_Version 104 + Parent_Hierarchy present -> entity file. Neither -> standalone BEJSON, not part of any MFDB. An entity whose Parent_Hierarchy points at a manifest that doesn't exist is an orphan -- invalid MFDB even though it's valid BEJSON 104 on its own. Example entity file (data/user.bejson): {\"Format\":\"BEJSON\",\"Format_Version\":\"104\",\"Format_Creator\":\"Elton Boehnen\",\"Parent_Hierarchy\":\"../104a.mfdb.bejson\",\"Records_Type\":[\"User\"],\"Fields\":[{\"name\":\"user_id\",\"type\":\"string\"},{\"name\":\"username\",\"type\":\"string\"},{\"name\":\"active\",\"type\":\"boolean\"}],\"Values\":[[\"U01\",\"alice\",true],[\"U02\",\"bob\",true]]}.",
      "keyword_only",
      true,
      3
    ],
    [
      "bejson_core_7",
      "MFDB does NOT enforce referential integrity -- FKs are convention only, two signals for tooling: field names ending _fk (e.g. user_id_fk), and/or a primary_key declaration in the manifest naming which field other entities' _fk fields point at. Tools should treat an unresolved FK as a warning, never a hard error. MFDB provides no join syntax -- joins happen in the app/tooling layer using those two signals; MFDB just gives them something to key off of. Validation is 3 tiers: Level 1 manifest (valid 104a, entity_name/file_path present+unique+non-null, every file_path resolves on disk) = hard failure if broken. Level 2 per-entity (valid 104, Parent_Hierarchy present and resolves, Records_Type matches its manifest entry, bidirectional path check both directions agree) = hard failure. Level 3 database-level (record_count drift, unresolved strict-mode FKs) = warning only, does not invalidate the database. Key practices: one manifest per database root, never nested; write atomically (temp file + rename) especially the manifest; append new entity fields only at the end (mid-array insert breaks positional integrity for every existing record); never plaintext secrets. MFDB is NOT a query engine and NOT a new Format_Version -- it's a naming/organization convention with a registry.",
      "keyword_only",
      true,
      3
    ],
    [
      "bejson_core_8",
      "This agent's own BEJSON tool tags: bejson_create (NEW file with a schema you specify -- rejects an invalid field type before writing anything, e.g. catches \"type\":\"Number\" instead of \"number\" up front; ALWAYS prefer this over write_file for a brand-new BEJSON file), bejson_fields (list schema), bejson_add_field (append field, null-pads existing rows), bejson_delete_field (remove field + its column from every row), bejson_create_record (append a row), bejson_delete_record (remove row by index), bejson_set_value (set one cell by row+field name or row+column index). All use the field-map cache for name lookups, not linear scans.",
      "keyword_only",
      true,
      1
    ],
