    [
      "bejson_core_9",
      "MFDB v1.31 Master-Slave federation: a manifest's Network_Role header (\"Master\" or \"Slave\") tags its role in a multi-node setup -- not a new key type, just a standard 104a custom header. Analogy: Master is HQ's central filing cabinet (the authoritative registry, full visibility into every Slave); a Slave is a satellite office desk -- 'structurally blind', no hardcoded path back to HQ, no read-access to HQ's archives, so its own local context stays lean. Two one-way data flows keep them in sync without the Slave needing awareness of the Master: (1) One-Way Push -- the Slave truncates/distills its own logs and pushes the summary up to the Master's polling directory; (2) Inverse Drop-Zone Polling -- when the Master needs to push a policy update, it writes a BEJSON 104a doc into the Slave's local directory using an atomic file-swap (os.rename, never a partial write), and the blind Slave just polls its OWN directory to pick it up -- it never reaches out to the Master directly. A ConnectedSlave registry entity on the Master side (label, url, role, status, supported_entities) tracks network topology. This is for multi-device/multi-node setups specifically -- a single local MFDB database has no Network_Role at all and doesn't need one.",
      "keyword_only",
      true,
      3
    ],
    [
      "behtml_13",
      "BEHTML Z-axis (added PKG25-27, not in the original spec): schema field z_index renamed to z_layer -- same position, new meaning. Collision now requires SAME X/Y position AND the SAME z_layer; different layers may deliberately share identical X/Y without colliding or even warning (a real behavior change from the old 'same z_index overlap = flagged' rule). CSS conversion is layer*32 (BEHTML_Z_LAYER_PX) via behtml_core_layer_to_css_zindex. Arrow-key canvas navigation stays within one layer only. lib_becss_zlayer.js is a SEPARATE, independent chrome-layering system (sidebar/drawer/dialog stacking discipline: dialogs stack freely among themselves and never force-close anything; sidebar and drawer are mutually exclusive with each other and force-close each other on open; neither force-closes a dialog; sidebar/drawer's own layer live-recalculates to one above the highest open dialog) -- it shares the same 32px-per-layer scale as BEHTML's z_layer but is not the same system and does not read/write z_layer values.",
      "keyword_only",
      true,
      2
    ],
    [
      "mfdb132_1",
      "MFDB-132 (Chunked-104a): collapses an entire federated MFDB database -- manifest plus every entity file -- into ONE portable BEJSON 104a document, for transport/session-mounting rather than the standard zipped 1.31 multi-file layout (1.31 is still correct for a live, non-diagram MFDB app; diagrams standardize on 1.32 specifically because a diagram is meant to end up as one file). Fixed schema: File_Name, File_Extension, File_Content, File_Version, File_Hash, Relative_Path, Is_Binary, Is_Mounted -- schema version hardcoded to 1.32 (MFDB_CHUNK_SCHEMA_VERSION). Cryptographic sticky mounts: MFDB132Archive.mount writes a .mfdb132_lock file; a future mount reuses the existing workspace instantly if the package's current SHA-256 still matches the lock's original_hash and the schema validates, instead of re-extracting. A commit runs full validation as a strict pre-write gate -- any corrupted entity rejects the whole commit before touching physical storage. Purely additive: does not change v1.31 disk layout, manifest structure, or core validation rules inside the active workspace.",
      "keyword_only",
      true,
      2
    ],
    [
      "field_map_1",
      "MANDATORY access pattern, not optional style: BEJSON's on-disk Fields/Values arrays ARE positionally aligned (that's the format's own storage guarantee -- Fields order == every row's order, exactly, like a spreadsheet header matching its columns). But CODE must NEVER read or write a field via a bare index like row[3] or row[-1] -- always resolve through the field map cache: bejson_core_get_field_map(doc) builds/caches a {field_name: index} dict from doc['Fields']; bejson_core_get_field_index(doc, field_name) resolves one name through that cache. Every Core helper (bejson_core_add_record, bejson_core_update_field, bejson_core_filter_rows, bejson_core_sort_by_field, etc.) already does this internally -- new code calling into a BEJSON document directly must too. A schema/field mismatch caught by name lookup fails loudly; the same mismatch caught by a bare positional index either throws an unrelated error or silently corrupts the wrong column (this exact bug -- a client doing its own fresh array+scan per field instead of a cached map, silently corrupting layout via row[-1] on a mismatch -- was a real fix in the BEHTML IDE's own Qwen3.8 audit remediation, 2026-08-26).",
      "keyword_only",
      true,
      3
    ],
    [
      "core_lib_1",
      "Library ecosystem layout: four parallel language runtimes (Lib_PY primary/most complete, Lib_JS, Lib_TS, Lib_SH), each with a Core family (atomic file I/O, field map cache, structural validator, MFDB orchestrator+validator, chunking/packaging engine for both 1.31 zip and 1.32 chunked) and a Core_Nesting family (the nested-BEJSON scanner/mutator, its own error range). Core is frozen/immutable per project policy -- build on top, don't edit in place, except by Elton's explicit instruction. Every other library (AI, CMS/ExpCMS, Gaming, HTML/MD/BEHTML, Chunker, Cognition, Diagrams, System, Utility, and any app-specific NewAgentCMS-style family) belongs to the app it was built for -- that app can modify its own libraries freely; it must not modify another app's or the shared Core family's.",
      "keyword_only",
      true,
      2
    ],
    [
      "core_lib_2",
      "Bootstrap convention for a project consuming these libraries: copy the Core family's files into that project's OWN lib/ folder (never import across project boundaries by relative-pathing into a sibling project's directory) and source/import Core from there -- e.g. sys.path insert of that project's own ./lib before `import lib_bejson_Core_bejson_core`. A project's app-specific libraries (its own family, e.g. lib_bejson_<ProjectName>_*) either live in that same lib/ folder alongside the copied Core files, or -- when a project has multiple sub-tools each needing their own app-specific libs but sharing one Core copy -- in a dedicated tools_lib/<tool-name>/ folder per tool, with the shared Core copy staying in the project's single top-level lib/. This second pattern (one shared lib/, per-tool tools_lib/<name>/) is what NewAgent itself uses for its own tools/NA-CMS: NA-CMS's CMS-specific libraries live in tools_lib/na-cms/, while it sources Core from NewAgent's shared top-level lib/ rather than keeping its own private copy -- keeping a private copy is exactly how NA-CMS's own path_guard was caught having silently drifted a full version behind the shared one during that reroute.",
      "keyword_only",
      true,
      2
    ],
